.TH INTERFACES 4 "30 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
interfaces \- how each network interface is set up at boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
says, for every network interface of the machine, whether it is set up and
how.
The installer writes it, the administrator edits it, and no package owns it.
.PP
Each line describes an interface, or is a global line.
A
.B #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line; blank lines are ignored.
Words are separated by blanks or tabs.
.SS Interface lines
.nf
\fIkey  mode\fP  [\fIaddress\fP[\fB/\fP\fIbits\fP]]  [\fBgw\fP \fIrouter\fP]  [\fBname\fP \fIhost\fP]
.fi
.TP
.I key
which interface the line is for:
an interface name such as
.BR aen0 ,
.B zen0
or
.BR hya0 ;
a station (MAC) address such as
.BR 00:80:51:01:02:04 ,
which matches whatever interface has that card, so one disk image can serve
several machines;
or
.BR * ,
any interface no other line names.
For an interface the line with its MAC address counts first, then the line
with its name, then
.BR * .
An interface no line matches is left down.
.TP
.I mode
.B static
sets the
.I address
given.
.B dhcp
asks a DHCP server for the address; an
.I address
given on the line is used if no server answers.
.B off
leaves the interface down; anything after it is ignored.
.TP
.IR address [ / bits ]
the interface's address, with the length of the network prefix in bits
(1 to 30).
Without
.IR bits ,
the old class of the address decides (8, 16 or 24).
.TP
.BI gw " router"
the default router, which must be on the interface's network.
.TP
.BI name " host"
the machine's name, written to
.B /etc/nodename
and to this host's line in
.BR /etc/inet/hosts .
Letters, digits and
.BR \- ,
at most 63.
.SS Global lines
.TP
.BI dns " address ..."
name servers to use. Several
.B dns
lines add up.
Name servers a DHCP server gives are added to these unless a
.B dns
line also says
.BR static .
.TP
.BI domain " name"
the domain of this host, used only for the full name in
.BR /etc/inet/hosts ;
it is never given to the resolver, which would append it to every lookup.
.TP
.B hostname dhcp
take the machine's name from the DHCP server.
Without this line a DHCP server never changes the name.
.SH ERRORS
A mistake in an interface line leaves that interface down, rather than
letting the
.B *
line take it.
A mistake in a global line causes the line to be ignored.
Every mistake is reported in the boot log, with its line number, when the
network is set up.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.ft CW
# /etc/inet/interfaces
zen0               dhcp
aen0               static  192.0.2.38/24  gw 192.0.2.1
00:80:51:01:02:04  static  192.0.2.39/24  gw 192.0.2.1  name tower
*                  off
dns     192.0.2.14 192.0.2.23
domain  example.com
.ft R
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
this file.
.TP
.B /etc/inet/link.d/*
the network drivers present, one file each, shipped by the driver packages.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dlpi-mac (1M),
.BR rc.conf (4)
